Bonaventrua Francesco Cavaliri 1598 was an Italian mathematican and a Jesuate. 
He is known for his work on the problems of optics and motion, work on indivisibles, the precursors of infinitesimals calculus, and the introduction of logarithms to Italy. 
Cavalieri's principle in goemetry partially anticipated integral calculus. 

Cavalieri joined the Jesuates order at the age of 15, taking the name Bonaventura upon becoming a novice of the order, and remained a member until his death. 
IN 1616 he was a student of geometry at the University of Pisa. 
There he came under the tutelage introduced himn to Galileo Galilie.

From 1632 to 1646, Cavalieri published eleven books dealing with problems in astronomy, optics, motion and geometry.

According to Gilles-Gaston Granger, Cavalieri belongs with Newton and Leibniz level to mathematical warrors of the 17th and 18th centuries.  
